  "clips": [
    {
      "id": "01",
      "title": "Not just deadlock",
      "question": "Most real-world concurrency bugs aren't deadlocks. What are they?",
      "scenes": [
        {
          "type": "question",
          "say": "Concurrency bugs, question one. Most concurrency bugs found in real software aren't deadlocks. So what are they? Pause and answer out loud.",
          "pauseAfter": 3,
          "props": {
            "question": "Most concurrency bugs aren't deadlocks. <em>What are they?</em>"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "say": "A study of real bugs in MySQL, Apache, Mozilla and OpenOffice found 105 concurrency bugs, and 74 of them were not deadlocks. Almost all of those were one of two kinds. An atomicity violation: the code assumes two steps happen together, but they don't. Here one thread checks a pointer, another thread nulls it, and the first thread uses it anyway. The fix is one lock, held around the check and the use, and around the other thread's write. And an order violation: the code assumes one thing happens before another. Here the new thread may read the thread pointer before its creator has stored it. The fix is to enforce the order: wait on a condition variable, until initialization is done.",
          "props": {
            "title": "Atomicity and <em>order</em>",
            "file": "bugs.c",
            "tag": "non-deadlock",
            "lang": "cpp",
            "size": 20,
            "width": 1210,
            "code": "// Atomicity violation (MySQL): check, then use, not atomically\nif (thd->proc_info)                       // T1: not NULL...\n    fputs(thd->proc_info, out);           // ...T2 nulls it in between: crash\n// Fix: one lock around the check and the use, and around T2's write\n\n// Order violation (Mozilla): assumes mThread is already set\nvoid init(void)  { mThread = PR_CreateThread(mMain, ...); }\nvoid mMain(void) { mState = mThread->State; }   // may run first: NULL\n// Fix: make the order explicit\npthread_mutex_lock(&m);\nwhile (!mtInit) pthread_cond_wait(&c, &m);      // init sets mtInit, signals\npthread_mutex_unlock(&m);",
            "highlights": [
              {
                "at": "together",
                "lines": "2-3",
                "note": "Check and use, not atomic"
              },
              {
                "at": "lock",
                "lines": "4",
                "note": "Fix: one lock around both sides"
              },
              {
                "at": "before",
                "lines": "7-8",
                "note": "Assumes init already ran"
              },
              {
                "at": "condition",
                "lines": "10-12",
                "note": "Fix: wait for the order"
              }
            ]
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "02",
      "title": "Deadlock",
      "question": "What four conditions must all hold for deadlock, and which is easiest to break?",
      "scenes": [
        {
          "type": "question",
          "say": "Question two. What four conditions must all hold for a deadlock? And which one is easiest to break? Pause and answer out loud.",
          "pauseAfter": 3,
          "props": {
            "question": "Four conditions for <em>deadlock</em>. Which do you break?"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "graph",
          "say": "Thread one takes lock A, then wants lock B. Thread two takes lock B, then wants lock A. Each holds what the other needs, so both wait forever. Four conditions must all hold. Mutual exclusion: a lock has one owner. Hold and wait: threads hold locks while waiting for more. No preemption: nobody can take a lock away. And circular wait: a cycle, each thread waiting on the next. Break any one, and deadlock can't happen. The practical one is the cycle. Always acquire locks in one global order, A before B, and no cycle can form.",
          "props": {
            "title": "A <em>cycle</em> of waits",
