    {
      "id": "03",
      "title": "Priority inversion",
      "question": "A high-priority thread waits on a lock while a medium-priority thread runs. What went wrong?",
      "scenes": [
        {
          "type": "question",
          "say": "Question three. A high priority thread is stuck waiting for a lock, and a medium priority thread is running. What went wrong? Pause and answer out loud.",
          "pauseAfter": 3,
          "props": {
            "question": "High waits, <em>Medium</em> runs. What went wrong?"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "graph",
          "say": "Low takes a lock. High wakes up, and needs the same lock, so it blocks, waiting for Low. Fine so far: Low should finish soon. But then Medium wakes up. It doesn't need the lock at all, but it outranks Low, so it preempts Low, and runs for as long as it likes. Now High is waiting on Medium, a lower priority thread. That's priority inversion. It famously kept resetting the Mars Pathfinder lander, in 1997. The fix is priority inheritance: while Low holds a lock that High wants, Low runs at High's priority, so Medium can't preempt it. Low finishes, releases the lock, and High runs.",
          "props": {
            "title": "Priority <em>inversion</em>",
            "nodes": [
