        {
          "type": "compare",
          "say": "Two standard fixes. Priority inheritance boosts the holder only while a higher priority thread is waiting. POSIX mutexes offer it as an attribute, and Linux implements it, but a plain standard mutex doesn't use it. A priority ceiling runs any holder of the lock at the highest priority of any thread that uses it, which also prevents some deadlocks. Or design it away: don't share locks across priority levels.",
          "props": {
            "title": "Inherit, or <em>ceiling</em>",
            "left": {
              "at": "boosts",
              "label": "Inheritance",
              "title": "Boost while someone waits",
              "tone": "good",
              "body": "The holder borrows the highest waiter's priority."
            },
            "right": {
              "at": "ceiling",
              "label": "Ceiling",
              "title": "Always run at the ceiling",
              "body": "Any holder runs at the lock's ceiling priority."
            },
            "verdict": {
              "at": "plain",
              "text": "std::mutex does neither: set a pthread mutex protocol.",
              "code": "PTHREAD_PRIO_INHERIT"
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "recap",
          "say": "To recap. Most concurrency bugs are atomicity or order violations: fix them with locks, and condition variables. Deadlock needs four conditions, and a global lock order breaks the cycle. And priority inversion is fixed by letting a lock holder inherit its waiter's priority.",
          "props": {
            "title": "Concurrency bugs, <em>in three lines</em>",
            "items": [
              {
                "at": "violations",
                "text": "Atomicity and order violations: locks, condition variables.",
                "code": "most real bugs"
